d Likely R all point to a comfortable Republican win, with markets agreeing at roughly 86% for Moore. Womack's minimal campaign resources leave little evidence to move the forecast closer, though the mixed expert ratings and dated, partisan polling keep this at Likely rather than Safe.", "watch": "A credible independent poll showing a single-digit race, or a major late scandal or funding surge for Womack, would be the main signals of movement.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tim Moore (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 14th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term incumbent Tim Moore is seeking re-election with a large financial edge. He has raised roughly $1.5 million and holds about $1.6 million on hand, while Democrat Lakesha Womack has raised under $25,000 and shows no outside spending on either side. Expert raters are split between Safe R and Likely R, and prediction markets give Moore roughly an 86% chance of winning.\n\nThe only available poll is a March survey from a Republican-sponsored firm showing Moore ahead by eight points, so the forecast rests mainly on fundamentals and ratings. Moore has maintained a steady stream of constituent-focused and legislative announcements, including disaster funding for the district. The race remains Likely Republican, with Womack facing steep structural and financial disadvantages.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0644, "margin": -9.66}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0543, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0528, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0536, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0567, "margin": -10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0411, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0477, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0512, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0